  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Preparation, design and implementation of two Fish Farmer Field Schools in Sidoarjo, Indonesia</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The coastal aquaculture landscape of Sidoarjo Regency, Indonesia, covers approximately 15,000 hectares and is dominated by fish and shrimp ponds. The conversion of former mangrove areas into aquaculture ponds has reduced water exchange, contributing to sediment accumulation in rivers and canals, upstream flooding, and changes in salinity levels that affect aquaculture productivity. A 2023 study on improving aquaculture systems in Sidoarjo identified mangrove conservation and expansion as key nature-based solutions (NBS) to improve water management, sediment transport, and ecosystem resilience.This project pilots two community-based Fish Farmer Field Schools (FFFS) in the aquaculture clusters of Sedati and Jabon. Unlike traditional training approaches, FFFS use participatory, farmer-centred learning to build local awareness, knowledge, and capacity to integrate NBS into aquaculture practices. The project will adapt the FFFS curriculum to the local context, including assessing opportunities to strengthen existing organic aquaculture systems affected by declining salinity levels.The project aims to increase the capacity of aquaculture communities and local institutions to apply NBS that improve both aquaculture production and water quality. Particular attention will be given to two pilot measures:1.	establishing large brackish-water storage and treatment ponds, and2.	converting sections of ponds along waterways into mangrove greenbelts.Lessons learned and knowledge products will be documented and disseminated to support wider uptake of NBS and silvofisheries approaches in Indonesia, contributing to national mangrove rehabilitation ambitions and strengthening landscape-based coastal management.</narrative>
  </description>
